WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.440
 This is a quick introduction to the semantic web.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.120
 Over the next couple of minutes I'll outline one of the toughest problems the web is facing

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.120
 today.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.960
 I'll also explain what's being done about it and how it's going to affect you.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.520
 You don't need to be a web developer or blogger to understand any of the concepts in this

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.720
 short intro.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.120
 If you can open a web browser, then you can understand the semantic web.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.920
 So before going any further, it's important to know what the word semantics actually means.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.920
 Semantics is related to syntax.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.260
 In most languages, syntax is how you say something, whereas semantics is the meaning

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.600
 behind what you said.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.380
 Let's take the phrase "I love technology" as an example.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:42.480
 The syntax is all of the letters, words, and punctuation in the sentence.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.820
 The semantics is what the sentence actually means.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.360
 In this case, it means that you enjoy learning about and using new technology.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.400
 Now if we were to change the sentence using a different symbol for the word "love", we

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.500
 have changed the syntax of the sentence.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.180
 However, note that the semantics of the sentence is still the same.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.440
 When you write "I heart technology", it still means that you enjoy learning about and using

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.860
 new technology.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:11.400
 When we talk about syntax and semantics, what we are really talking about is communication.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.180
 When you want to communicate with somebody else, you use your voice to do so.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.120
 The internet created a standard way for computers to communicate with one another.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.860
 In other words, it gave a voice to computers so that they may talk to each other and exchange

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.200
 information.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.720
 However, much like a parrot can mimic human sounds without understanding them, computers

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.620
 merely mimic human information to one another.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:36.060
 So while the internet enables computers to talk to one another, it was not designed to teach

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.760
 them what the information actually means.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.880
 When the web came along, it created a very quick and easy way for us to retrieve and view

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:45.880
 information.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.340
 The web is a huge document storage and retrieval system.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.100
 When you put a website address into your browser, it sends a request to the website.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.800
 The request basically states that you would like the document located at the address that

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.020
 you gave it.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.600
 The website retrieves the document and sends it back to your web browser.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.760
 This document is written in a language called HTML.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.040
 The HTML language defines a syntax that computers can understand.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.520
 It tells the computer how to display the document to you.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:16.540
 So the two really neat things that the web did is create a way to get any document on

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:22.400
 the internet, and it also created a syntax called HTML that is used to display the document

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.480
 for you.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.840
 So what's the big deal?

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.400
 We have the internet that lets us talk to each other.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.120
 We have the web that lets us store and retrieve any document on the internet.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.280
 And we have search engines which can find just about any website that we'd want.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.240
 The web is already pretty good, so how are we going to make it any better?

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.700
 The answer lies in semantics.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.800
 Remember, computers today just blindly retrieve and show us information.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.240
 That's the problem.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.360
 Computers don't understand the meaning behind the web pages that they're showing us.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.440
 While they may understand the syntax, the semantics are lost on them.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.780
 Now if we could get computers to recognize what's in a web page, they could learn what we're

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.080
 interested in.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.560
 If they know that, then they can help us get what we want.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.980
 They would change from passively helping us to actively helping us.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.820
 This is really what the semantic web is all about.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.340
 It helps computers understand the meaning behind a web page.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.720
 The web of today is about documents, whereas the semantic web is about things.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:29.480
 When I say things, I mean anything: people, places, events, music, movies, organizations,

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.460
 and just about any concept that you can think of.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.680
 The semantic web is not only about pointing these things out to a computer, but also about

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.720
 letting computers know how these things are related to each other.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.600
 There are several promising technologies that are in use today that can embed semantics in

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.880
 HTML documents.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.880
 Two of the more popular ways are called microformats and RDFa.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.300
 So the semantic web stuff isn't about something that is going to happen in the future.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.520
 It's about something that is happening today.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.560
 Things get really exciting when we start to explore the possibilities of the semantic web.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.400
 Once your computer can understand what a person, a place, and an event is, it can start helping

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.400
 you interact with those things.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.620
 For example, if a birthday party is marked up as an event with a date and a place, you can

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.960
 tell your computer to save the date in your calendar.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:22.820
 Another example is in the world of music blogs.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.540
 Music blogs usually list songs and album reviews on their front page.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.460
 If the blog marked up the songs and the artists using semantic technology, you could tell your

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.140
 browser to find all of the songs on the page, or search the internet for other albums by the

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.040
 same artist.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.280
 Search engines would also become a great deal more accurate than they are today.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.660
 When you search, you could say that you're searching for a person, a place, or a particular

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:48.660
 song.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.800
 A search engine could then refer you to a website with far more accuracy because it wouldn't

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.580
 have to just depend on keywords in web pages anymore.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.480
 It could also depend on the semantics in that web page.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.120
 So the semantic web holds a great deal of promise in making our lives easier by helping

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.860
 computers help us get what we want.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:13.540
 If you'd like to find out more about the semantic web, there are a couple of websites that are

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.120
 good starting points.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.640
 If you'd like to know what all of this looks like in a web browser, check out the operator

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.780
 plugin for the Firefox web browser.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:25.760
 There's also a group of people on the web called the microformats community that are working

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:29.020
 on spreading the idea of the semantic web.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:33.920
 Last but not least, the semantic web is also a major focus of the World Wide Web Consortium.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:38.660
 They are the people that are working on HTML and RDFa and many of the other technologies

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:44.240
 that power the semantic web today and into the future.

